require "emo/ui"
require "emo/ui_fb"

// The first frame (emo-desktop A7.2, A8.3): the tree the machine's
// screen pass paints first, and the digest the golden's eye reads off
// any surface. Since A8.3 the first frame is the counter's — the view
// at the initial model, the frame the Elm loop's `mount` paints before
// the first event (`counter.emo`) — and `tree` is that tree as a value,
// for the probe that measures it and the reader who wants the first
// picture in one line. Three readings answer the one number — the
// pass's own read-back of the carve it painted, the hosted canvas's
// render of the same tree (`golden/`), and the monitor's screendump of
// the display (`run.sh`) — or the frame is not on the screen.
//
// The picture is the counter's panel over the whole display — the box
// model lays the root into whatever rectangle the surface is, so the
// width and height are the display's answer, never a constant here —
// with the count and the two buttons inside its padding, in M8's
// glyphs. M7's first picture, the title strip, the body and the dock,
// cost the metal 42 KiB of boxed numbers per frame; the panel's is the
// size the arena keeps a session of (`counter.emo`).

def tree() ui.Node {
  return counter.view(ui.Registry.new(), counter.initial())
}

// Every pixel in row-major order folded into one 32-bit number,
// `d = (d * 31 + word) mod 2^32`. Order-sensitive — a frame shifted by
// one pixel digests to something else — and integer arithmetic every
// target and the golden's Python reproduce exactly, so the readings
// agree on one number or on none.
def digest(s ui_fb.Surface) Int64 {
  var d = 0
  for var y = 0; y < s.height(); y = y + 1 {
    for var x = 0; x < s.width(); x = x + 1 {
      d = (d * 31 + s.at(x, y)) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    }
  }
  return d
}
